  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "The sides of a parallelogram have lengths $60\\text{ cm}$ and $25\\text{ cm}$ and the length of one of the diagonals is $65\\text{ cm}$. Find the area of the parallelogram.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$1500\\text{ cm}^2$.\n\nUse Heron’s formula for a triangle with sides $60$, $25$ and $65\\text{ cm}$, then double its area.",
      "worked": "The diagonal divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles with sides $60$, $25$ and $65\\text{ cm}$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}s&=\\frac{60+25+65}{2}\\\\s&=75\\text{ cm}\\\\A_{\\triangle}&=\\sqrt{s(s-60)(s-25)(s-65)}\\\\A_{\\triangle}&=\\sqrt{75\\times15\\times50\\times10}\\\\A_{\\triangle}&=750\\text{ cm}^2\\\\A_{\\text{parallelogram}}&=2\\times750\\\\A_{\\text{parallelogram}}&=1500\\text{ cm}^2\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 76
  },
